{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XPeBWEj2TiLiCwCWtbzRKo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UxFKmowXt74XaBsM2qHnKm","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment: R1 — Methods & Experiments\n\nYou are reviewing: **\"Differential Depth Response in Non-Paraphrastic LLM Generation Chains: An Empirical Characterization Across Model Families\"**\n\n**Your focus**: Methods, experimental design, statistical rigor, and results interpretation.\n\n**Paper summary**: The paper formulates \"generation depth classification\" — classifying text as gen-0 (human), gen-1 (first-generation LLM output), or gen-2+ (recursively generated) — for non-paraphrastic continuation chains. The core empirical finding is \"differential depth response\" (DDR): 7 features extracted from a reference model's NLL distribution behave differently across depth. Central-tendency/diversity features (entropy_mean, TTR, hapax_ratio) drift monotonically with depth (|ρ|>0.60), while shape features (entropy_max, tail_decay) remain near-stationary (|ρ|<0.10). entropy_std is identified as \"intermediate\" (d=0.458, TOST rejection 0/1000). A 7-feature XGBoost ensemble achieves binary F1=0.694 under 8-generator LOGO, 0.830 on cross-model chains. Downstream utility experiment shows depth-aware curation is equivalent to random subsampling (TOST p=1.1e-4 at ±2%).\n\n**Key tables/results to evaluate**:\n- Table 1: Feature ablation (full 0.820 vs surface-only 0.763 vs reference-only 0.719)\n- Table 2: Feature trend classification (Spearman ρ and KS by feature)\n- Table 3: LOGO per-generator results (binary F1 range 0.659–0.724)\n- Table 4: LOGO vs LOFO (Δ = -0.008 binary)\n- Table 5: Cross-reference-model validation (3 ref models, LOGO F1 0.803–0.816)\n- Table 6: Baseline comparison (7-feat 0.798 vs Avg-LogProb 0.765 vs 4-det ensemble 0.795)\n- Table 7: Cross-model chains (0.830 vs single-model 0.694)\n- Table 8: Downstream CLM perplexity (negative finding, TOST equivalence)\n- Table 9: 9-generator LOGO (Gemma-2-2B anomaly: gen-1 F1=0.036)\n- Appendix tables: 5-class ordinal (F1=0.50), human edit robustness, LD-Score, QLoRA (0.889–0.907 3-class), length control, temperature sensitivity, instruct degradation, TOST bootstrap, causality\n\n**Evaluation criteria** (top ML venue, ~25% accept, 6-point scale):\n1. Soundness: Are the experimental designs valid? Are there confounds? Are statistical tests appropriate?\n2. Completeness: Are key experiments present? What's missing?\n3. Results interpretation: Are claims proportional to evidence? Any over-claims?\n4. Reproducibility: Sufficient detail for replication?\n\n**Specific questions to address**:\n- Is the LOGO/LOFO evaluation protocol sound for leak-free evaluation?\n- Is binary F1=0.694 a strong enough headline result?\n- Is the baseline comparison (Table 6) fair? The 7-feature advantage over Avg-LogProb is only +3.3pp.\n- How should we interpret the QLoRA gap (+12–15pp over handcrafted features)?\n- Is the downstream negative finding (3 seeds, single metric) sufficiently powered?\n- Is the Gemma-2-2B anomaly (gen-1 F1=0.036) adequately addressed?\n- Are the scope limitations (base models only, t<0.9) too restrictive for practical relevance?\n\n**Experiment coverage check**: The review context lists 20 imagined experiments. Check which are covered in the paper and which are missing or underreported.\n\nPlease provide:\n1. A structured review with strengths and weaknesses\n2. Specific scores for: Soundness, Significance, Clarity (each 1-6)\n3. An overall assessment score (1-6) with justification\n4. Questions for the authors","reviewer_id":"1","round_number":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1516,"cache_read_input_tokens":69123,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1516},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f241990-8f14-4f6d-9955-64995179e409","uuid":"2d8b248b-7aae-4be0-a947-70848ad1074e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UxFKmowXt74XaBsM2qHnKm","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_999770e4e57bfc6eabd12fc1f64af1ef:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f241990-8f14-4f6d-9955-64995179e409","uuid":"c3bebf7b-f915-4700-9c0e-ba115a728776","timestamp":"2026-05-20T10:51:25.439Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_999770e4e57bfc6eabd12fc1f64af1ef:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}","structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\": true, \"data\": {\"reviewer_key\": \"feishu:group:oc_999770e4e57bfc6eabd12fc1f64af1ef:reviewer_1\"}, \"error\": \"\"}"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XPeBWEj2TiLiCwCWtbzRKo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y5wbAnHFxBjJ2w2BDFFJU3","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment: R2 — Novelty & Related Work\n\nYou are reviewing: **\"Differential Depth Response in Non-Paraphrastic LLM Generation Chains: An Empirical Characterization Across Model Families\"**\n\n**Your focus**: Novelty, positioning against prior work, conceptual contribution, and broader impact.\n\n**Paper summary**: The paper formulates \"generation depth classification\" — classifying text as gen-0 (human), gen-1 (first-generation LLM output), or gen-2+ (recursively generated) — for non-paraphrastic continuation chains. The core empirical finding is \"differential depth response\" (DDR): 7 features extracted from a reference model's NLL distribution behave differently across depth. Drifting features (entropy_mean, TTR, hapax_ratio, |ρ|>0.60) capture progressive vocabulary impoverishment; shape features (entropy_max, tail_decay, |ρ|<0.10) remain stationary. entropy_std is \"intermediate.\" The paper validates across 8 base-model generators from 5 families, compares against existing detectors, and shows a negative downstream utility finding.\n\n**Related work covered in paper** (Section 2):\n- Perplexity-based detection: DetectGPT, Fast-DetectGPT, GLTR, Binoculars, DNA-GPT\n- Model collapse / iterative generation: Shumailov et al., \"LLM as Broken Telephone\" (ACL 2025)\n- Data quality / vocabulary diversity: stylometry, TTR, hapax in authorship attribution\n- OOD detection / membership inference\n\n**Key contribution claims**:\n1. Task formulation: ordinal depth classification beyond binary detection\n2. DDR characterization: drifting-vs-stationary spectrum with empirical validation\n3. Negative finding: depth fingerprinting is diagnostic, not curative\n\n**Evaluation criteria** (top ML venue, ~25% accept, 6-point scale):\n1. Novelty: Is the depth classification formulation genuinely new? Is DDR a meaningful conceptual contribution beyond \"vocabulary impoverishment gets worse with more generation\"?\n2. Positioning: Is the paper properly situated against (a) model collapse literature, (b) binary detection methods, (c) vocabulary diversity/stylometry work?\n3. Significance: Does this work open new research directions or is it an incremental extension of known phenomena?\n4. Completeness of related work: Any critical missing references?\n\n**Specific questions to address**:\n- Is DDR really novel, or is it a restatement of known vocabulary impoverishment / model collapse phenomena in a slightly different framing?\n- The \"LLM as Broken Telephone\" (ACL 2025) paper studies iterative generation chains with paraphrasing — how does this paper differentiate sufficiently?\n- The paper acknowledges that features overlap with OOD detection — is the contribution then primarily the ordinal formulation? Is that enough?\n- The negative downstream finding (depth curation ≈ random) — is this actually a contribution or a limitation? The paper frames it as establishing \"diagnostic not curative\" — is this framing convincing?\n- Base-model-only scope: given that most deployed LLMs are instruction-tuned, does this undermine the practical significance?\n- The 7-feature ensemble is outperformed by QLoRA (+12–15pp) — does this weaken the methodological contribution?\n- Binary F1=0.694 with per-generator range 0.633–0.724 — is this practical performance level meaningful?\n\n**References to check**:\n- Mitchell et al. 2023 (DetectGPT) — ICML\n- Bao et al. 2024 (Fast-DetectGPT) — ICLR\n- Gehrmann et al. 2019 (GLTR) — ACL\n- Hans et al. 2024 (Binoculars) — ICML\n- Yang et al. 2024 (DNA-GPT) — ICLR\n- Shumailov et al. (model collapse)\n- \"LLM as Broken Telephone\" (ACL 2025)\n- Any missing references the paper should cite\n\nPlease provide:\n1. A structured review with strengths and weaknesses\n2. Specific scores for: Novelty, Significance, Clarity (each 1-6)\n3. An overall assessment score (1-6) with justification\n4. Questions for the authors","reviewer_id":"2","round_number":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1516,"cache_read_input_tokens":69123,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1516},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f241990-8f14-4f6d-9955-64995179e409","uuid":"58d1c2aa-44d5-4ce0-954d-7c55e0607f39"}
